WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:04.710 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  mexico city's metropolitan area  is home to over 21 million people for  decades it has been plagued by  increasing overpopulation  and serious air pollution its streets  are gridlocked access to drinkable water  is scarce  and left unattended the city is expected  to dry out within the next 30 years  crime is on the rise and the gap between  the rich and the poor  is massive and only increasing abject  poverty is rampant  but there's a new mayor in town and she  has  big plans for its future i hope to solve  many many problems  the city it's innovation innovation for  me is the use of science and technology  to change  the way things work  scheimbaum is the first woman to be  elected mayor of mexico city  but that's not necessarily the most  interesting part of her story  because she's not just an ordinary  politician she's a nobel peace  prize-winning scientist  with an ambitious vision for this  troubled metropolis  my mother she is also a scientist she's  a biologist by biochemistry it was very  important for me in my formation  i have a master and a phd in energy  engineering  i always like math and nature  most of my academic life i studied  climate change and  energy the relation with energy and  climate change  alongside her strong scientific  background young claudia was raised to  be passionate about politics  politics has been always part of my  family  our conversations in one sense science  teach you  to question everything in politics it's  very very important to understand  why it's something happening  how to convince people of the ideas that  you think  are the real ideas to change your  country  scheinbaum pressed on with political  activism while thriving in her academic  career in energy engineering  for two decades in the year 2000 she  served her first public appointment  as the secretary of the environment of  mexico city  she jointly received the nobel peace  prize in 2007  as a member of the intergovernmental  panel on climate change  then later became mayor of mexico city's  borough slalopan from 2015 to 2017.  the following year she was elected mayor  of the whole city  today her passion for science and  political action has culminated  into a six-year plan to fundamentally  transform mexico city  into a cleaner more equitable place for  its 21 million inhabitants  i want for my city in the six years that  i'm going to be  the chief of government to develop  the access to rights not only education  health  but also culture sports  environment  the city is very unequal what i want for  the city is to reduce these  inequalities how to do that access to  rights  where they don't have and a better  environment  including resources to the poorest part  of the city  the mayor's grand vision includes seven  primary goals  reducing air pollution by 30 percent  planting  15 million trees banning single-use  plastics and promoting recycling  building a new waste separation plant  for the city  providing water service to every home  constructing a hundred kilometers of  corridors for public transportation  and constructing and installing solar  water heaters and solar panels city-wide  so that's a lot but despite the scale of  these ambitions  mayor scheinbaum has aligned herself  with mexico's president lopez obrador  as an advocate for drastic cuts in  government spending  you cannot have a rich government and a  poor people so what we did is  reduce government then you have a lot of  resources  for infrastructure and also for  different actions  we built a new university in the city in  two years  you need a lot of money where do you get  this money we didn't increase  taxes so you get this money reducing the  government  and the other thing is stop corruption  we feel like a citizen governing  citizens  we don't have any luxuries in our  government and this brings you a lot of  resources to bring to the people and to  reduce inequalities  hopefully you know in these six years i  can change  many things in the city public  transportation build parks  have digital access to many people  if you are able to change people  and to give opportunity to young people  then the city is going to be different  for the next years  mexico city has been hit hard by covid  19 with over 156  000 cases and 15 000 deaths as of  october of 2020  and while lofty government plans have  often fallen to the wayside this year  mayor scheinbaum has managed to  implement parts of her vision while  handling the public health crisis  this is best seen on the avenue of the  insurgents  mexico city's largest and busiest street  where bike lanes popped up  just as fast as the traffic died down  from quarantine  it's a very commercial street we're  asking restaurants and  business and everybody if they agree to  keep it  i want for everybody to be part of this  so that's what we're doing right now  still there is little doubt that 2020  was not the kind of start that the mayor  had hoped for  for her administration but as mexico  city's first female mayor  she is used to some obstacles and  overcoming them  has become somewhat of a preoccupation  of hers  mexico has the culture of machismo you  can feel as a woman that you're less  that you're not able to do anything  because everybody tells you  you're less than this man and as long as  you think that you're able to do it  it brings you the the strength to do it  paseo de la reforma is one of the main  avenues  you have many monuments they are only  men  right now we are building the women  heroines  it's the rescuing of women in history  it only makes sense that mayor  scheinbaum will face opposition against  her ambitious vision for mexico city  but her faith in science and its ability  to address  inequality and corruption in her city to  the benefit of her countrymen  remains unshakable  we believe in social change by  convincing people  we believe that mexico has a great  future  we end corruption in the country and we  believe that  education health and housing are rights  they are not merchandise  [Music]  you

